 \begin{abstract}
-The Rub' al-Khali (Empty Quarter) is the world's largest continuous sand desert, and its soil microbiome has not been surveyed across its vast landscape or over repeated visits. We surveyed microbial communities at $60$ sites along a west to east transect through the Empty Quarter in Saudi Arabia during $5$ campaigns over $31$ months. At each site, we sampled surface, shallow-subsurface, and root-adjacent soil, and obtained $1{,}237$ $16$S rRNA profiles together with climate, soil pH, and soil chemical composition data. Microbial community composition changed across the landscape, where dissimilarity increased with geographic distance in all sampled compartments. Replacement of taxa between sounts accounted to most of the dissimilarity in taxon presence and absence. Within sites, root-adjacent soil had the lowest alpha diversity and harbored communities distinct from bulk soil. Climate, soil pH, and soil chemical comoposition changed along the transect along with community composition. Exploratory models associated higher richness with rainfall in the days before sample collection. Predicted metabolic pathways followed the same geographic and compartment patterns. This survey and its linked data resource provide a baseline for the Empty Quarter soil microbiome and a framework for targeted environmental measurements and repeated surveys in the region.
