 \section*{Results}
 \subsection*{Pseudomonadota, Bacillota and Actinomycetota dominate Empty Quarter soils}
 
-We surveyed 60 sites along a west to east transect through the Empty Quarter in Saudi Arabia during 5 campaigns over 31 months (Figure~\ref{fig:landscape}a). At each site, we sampled bulk surface soil, shallow-subsurface soil and root-adjacent soil, collecting three field replicates per compartment. We sequenced the V3--V4 region of the bacterial $16$S rRNA gene and resolved the reads into amplicon sequence variants (ASVs). This yielded $1{,}227$ samples from the repeatedly sampled sites 1--60 (Figure~\ref{fig:landscape}b). Trip~$2$ covered only 8 sites due to extreme heat.
+We surveyed 60 sites along a west to east transect through the Empty Quarter in Saudi Arabia during 5 campaigns over 31 months (Figure~\ref{fig:landscape}a). At each site, we sampled bulk surface soil, shallow-subsurface soil and root-adjacent soil, collecting three field replicates per compartment. We sequenced the V3--V4 region of the bacterial $16$S rRNA gene and resolved the reads into amplicon sequence variants (ASVs). This yielded $1{,}237$ samples, $1{,}227$ of them from the repeatedly sampled sites 1--60 (Figure~\ref{fig:landscape}b). Trip~$2$ covered only 8 sites due to extreme heat.
 
-We assessed contamination at different study stages, and found that candidate contaminant ASVs comprised $\le 2\%$ of pooled reads and their removal did not affect Shannon diversity or richness ($\rho = 0.99$). Samples in adjacent wells during preparation were no more similar than matched samples in non-adjacent wells (one-sided $p=0.87$). Mock community controls varied in their recovery of expected genera. We therefore retained all ASVs in downstream analyses (Supplementary Section~\ref{sup:controls}).
