-We first describe the overall microbial community composition of the $1{,}227$ samples. Pseudomonadota was the most abundant phylum ($26.9\,\%$), followed by Bacillota ($22.5\,\%$) and Actinomycetota ($18.7\,\%$). Acidobacteriota and Verrucomicrobiota, which are among the most abundant phyla in global soil surveys \cite{delgadobaquerizo2018global,fierer2017embracing}, together accounted for $3.3\,\%$. At the genus level, \textit{Domibacillus} ($6.2\,\%$) was most abundant in every compartment, followed by \textit{Massilia} ($3.5\,\%$), \textit{Bacillus} ($3.0\,\%$), \textit{Microvirga} ($2.4\,\%$) and \textit{Flavisolibacter} ($2.0\,\%$). ASVs without a genus assignment made up $35.6\,\%$ of reads per sample on average (Supplementary section~\ref{sup:composition}).
