 \subsection*{Microbial communities change across the landscape}
 
-To test whether community composition changed across the landscape, we modelled the composition of each site, based on the $200$ most abundant genera, as a function of its position along the transect, allowing for either a steady west to east change or a change that peaks or troughs mid-transect. Transect position explained $40.2\,\%$ of the variation in composition among the $60$ sites ($95\,\%$ CI, $32.5$--$47.8\,\%$), compared with $25.5\,\%$ for a steady west--east trend alone. This result held when we changed the number of genera included, used ASVs instead of genera, or omitted any one campaign (Supplementary Section~\ref{sup:beta_transect}). However, the model residuals showed significant spatial autocorrelation (Moran's $I=0.064$, $p=0.001$), so sites could not be treated as independent, which inflates the significance of permutation tests. When we accounted for this spatial autocorrelation, the trend remained significant if autocorrelation was assumed to be short-range, but not if it was assumed to extend over hundreds of kilometres (Supplementary Section~\ref{sup:beta_spatialerrors}). We therefore report the variance explained as a descriptive measure of how strongly composition aligns with the transect, rather than evidence for a statistically supported west to east gradient.
