 \subsection*{Predicted functional potential follows geography and compartment}
 
-We predicted the functional potential of each sample from its $16$S profile with PICRUSt2 \cite{douglas2020picrust2} (Section~\ref{sec:function_methods}). Because prediction accuracy declines as sampled taxa become more distant from sequenced genomes, which is a particular concern for desert soils \cite{li2023capturing}, we first assessed prediction quality. The median weighted nearest-sequenced-taxon index (NSTI) was $0.165$, and was lowest in root-adjacent soil ($0.134$) and highest in shallow-subsurface soil ($0.201$; Supplementary Section~\ref{sup:function_prediction}). We then compared the predictions with KO profiles derived from metagenome-assembled genomes (MAGs) recovered from $125$ paired shotgun metagenomes. Across $3{,}471$ shared KOs, predicted and MAG-derived profiles were strongly correlated within samples (median Spearman $\rho=0.74$, $95\,\%$ CI $0.73$--$0.75$) and between community-average profiles ($\rho=0.81$; Figure~\ref{fig:function-controls}b). MAGs recruited on average $28.5\,\%$ of shotgun reads, so this comparison evaluates the predictions against the recovered fraction of each community (Supplementary Section~\ref{sup:function_validation}).
