-The graographic pattern coincided with gradients in climate, pH, and chemical composition. The eastern end of the transect was the warmest, most humid, and most alkaline, and was enriched in alkaliphilic and halophilic Bacillota such as \textit{Halalkalibacter}, \textit{Aquibacillus} and \textit{Sediminibacillus}, whereas Actinomycetota such as \textit{Cellulomonas} and \textit{Marmoricola} declined eastward. Soil pH accounted for part of the transect variation in composition, but did not explain variation between samples from the same site, suggesting that its association with composition is more prominent at the landscape scale. This is consistent with the role of pH as one of the main correlates of soil bacterial composition across biomes \cite{fierer2006diversity}. The elemental axis, reflecting carbonate, sulfate, and chloride minerals relative to quartz, explained less than $1\,\%$ of compositional variation within sites. Alpha diversity decreased with long term temperature, humidity, and rainfall, which runs counter to the expectation that more moist desert soil supports more diverse communities \cite{xiao2023influence}. Because these climate variables, pH and elemental composition all change along the same transect, our design cannot separate their individual effects, and the associations identify a shared environmental gradient rather than its drivers.
