-Within sites, compartments varied consistently in composition, with the largest and most consistent differences between root-adjacent soil and both bulk soil compartments. Vertical stratification has also been reported in an Atacama soil profile and between surface and subsurface Mojave biocrust communities \cite{horstmann2024atacama,pombubpa2020mojave}. Surface soil was enriched in pigmented, desiccation-tolerant genera such as \textit{Deinococcus} and \textit{Rubrobacter}, and shallow-subsurface soil in nitrifiers such as \textit{Nitrospira} and MND1. Root-adjacent soil was enriched in plant-associated genera, including  \textit{Neorhizobium}, \textit{Devosia} and \textit{Pseudomonas}, whereas the root-nodule genus \textit{Bradyrhizobium} was depleted. Root-adjacent soil had the lowest alpha diversity per sample, the highest dispersion among sites, largest core of shared genera, and slowest distance decay. Individual root-adjacent samples therefore contained smaller and less even subsets of a regional pool that was more widely shared across the landscape than the pools of bulk soil. Studies that identified desert plants have found host- and compartment-specific recruitment of root-associated bacteria \cite{philippot2013roots,eida2018desertplants,marasco2022namib,maurice2024alula}. Our root-adjacent compartment includes soil near and attached to the roots of unidentified plants, so these patterns describe the compartment as sampled. Sampling identified hosts at measured distances from roots would connect them to host identity and rhizosphere processes.
