 \subsection*{A regional framework for the Arabian Peninsula}
 
-Earlier studies in the Arabian Peninsula established habitat-specific patterns at individual locations. An inland sabkha in the United Arab Emirates hosted a saline microbial mat \cite{mckay2016sabkha}, and studies in Saudi Arabia examined highland soils \cite{yasir2015elevation}, arid sites \cite{khan2020saudi}, soil cursts \cite{aslam2016rainfall}, and the root microbiomes of desert plants \cite{eida2018desertplants}. Our survey contributes an additional scale to this work; a landscape-scale transect with repeated sampling of three soil compartments over five campaigns.
+Earlier studies in the Arabian Peninsula established habitat-specific patterns at individual locations. An inland sabkha in the United Arab Emirates hosted a saline microbial mat \cite{mckay2016sabkha}, and studies in Saudi Arabia examined highland soils \cite{yasir2015elevation}, arid sites \cite{khan2020saudi}, soil crusts \cite{aslam2016rainfall}, and the root microbiomes of desert plants \cite{eida2018desertplants}. Our survey contributes an additional scale to this work; a landscape-scale transect with repeated sampling of three soil compartments over five campaigns.
 
-We found that soil in the open Empty Quarter is alkaline, which falls within the range reported for soils in Saudi Arabia \cite{khan2020saudi}. At AlUla, soil beneath fertility islands formed by desert plants hhosted bacterial communities from those in the surrounding bulk soil, which was also more alkaline \cite{maurice2023fertility}. In the Empty Quarter, pH increased from west to east, and acounnted for part of the geographic variation in community composition, but did not explain compositional differences among samples from the same site. Therefore, pH may have a more pronounce effect at the landscape scale, rather than among neighbouring soil communities. Paired sampling of patches with vegetation and surrounding bulk soil would test whether local pH differences, such as those observed in AlUla, also shape composition at the scale of individual plants.
