-Our survey also provides a baseline for biodiversity monitoring in restoration in the region, and three of our results inform monitoring design. First, because turnover accounted for most of the dissimilarity between sites, no single site represents the regional community, and monitoring should retain broad geographic coverage. Second, because the three compartments differed in composition and alpha diversity, they should be samples separately. Third, because root-associated communities had the highest dispersion among sites, they may require more replicates than bulk soil to detect change. Around rainfall events, the first days after rain offer a candidate window for additional sampling. The linked samples, environmental measurements, sequences, and analysis inputs make these comparisons reusable \cite{rubalkhali_datapaper}.
